
  [1mB. ES modules[0m
  [38;5;153msettings.js[39m with [38;5;153mexport const apiEndpoint[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  imports it.
  [3mBuys:[0m a real module boundary, no globals, no ordering dependency. Closer to how you'd write this today.
  [3mCosts:[0m [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules, so the page stops working when opened directly as a file — you'd
  need a local server to develop. That's a real workflow change for a page that currently opens with a double-click.

  [1mC. Dual-target module[0m
  [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m written to work both as a browser global and as a CommonJS export, so the Node-side [38;5;153msrc/[39m tooling could
  [38;5;153mrequire()[39m it.
  [3mBuys:[0m one settings file usable from both module worlds already in this repo.
  [3mCosts:[0m UMD-style boilerplate serving a need that doesn't exist — [38;5;153msrc/[39m is unrelated to the webapp and makes no API
  calls. YAGNI.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m B is the better-engineered answer in the abstract, but the cost lands in exactly the wrong place: it
  trades a working double-click-the-file workflow for module hygiene on a four-file static page. A is honest about what
  this repo is, and if a build step ever arrives, converting the map to an ESM export is a few minutes' work. C solves a
  problem you don't have.

  One behavior question that goes with it: what should happen when the page is served from a hostname that isn't in the
  map?
